<style>
  /* Scoped project theme — remaps ONLY the semantic colour aliases within this
     page's subtree, so Wendy's branding can never leak into global scope.
     Everything structural (type scale, spacing rhythm, breakpoints) stays on
     the global tokens.

     Rojo is the brand's loudest voice and what the wordmark is drawn in, so it
     is `primary`; Celeste rules the construction grid and draws the concept
     rings, which is the job `accent` does on every other project page. */
  .project-wendys {
    --color-primary: #cd2028; /* Rojo Wendy's */
    --color-accent: #029cd4; /* Celeste Wendy's */

    --color-surface: #ffffff;
    --color-text: #000000; /* the artboard sets labels and headings in black */
    --color-muted: #6b6b6b; /* secondary copy */
    --color-border: #fcdbd8; /* the palette's own pale pink, as a hairline */

    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  /* No rule between the brief's meta list and its Proyecto / Desafío /
     Propuesta triad — the artboard doesn't have one, the same call Merienda,
     Vibra, Nacida y Criada and Combustion make. ProjectBrief draws a full-bleed
     hairline there for the older projects.

     The margin is kept: it is the gap between the two beats, and only the LINE
     is being removed. `:global()` is required because the <hr> is ProjectBrief's
     own markup, which Astro's scoping can't reach from here — the parent still
     carries this page's scope hash, so nothing leaks to another project. */
  .project-wendys :global(hr) {
    border-top-width: 0;
  }
</style>
